d for tahv la^ 'mule' (= Lh. th»ø-l\a 'rabbit') and lahv 'rabbit' (= Lh. l—ø 'mule')]. However, according to Delmos Jones (1967) the Lahu cycle begins with l»a-ni and ends with n»u-ni (for 'rabbit day' Jones records t—ø-ni instead of th»ø-l\a=ni) [cf. th»ø (Bn)]. The author independently confirmed this order in a RL village in Aug. 1977. The cycle is no longer remembered in any kind of order in X'n villages in Thailand, which have adopted the 7-day week [cf. «s|î (N; Clf)], though people are apparently still named for calendrical animals [cf. c\a (Bn), na (Bn)]. See chï-ni (N) under chï (N) 'barking-deer'; also \ø-l|o (N), l|o (Mpfx; Clf). In the original Chinese system, Rat-Day comes first.note	prob. & cø (V) [q.v.] 'go around'l for the voiced initial, cf. WB gya≥ 'top'; see kh—ø (N)..hw 	jø-|‰pos 	Ngl  	("little jø") the 12-day cycle..hw 	jø-l|opos 	Ngl  	("big jø") a 14-day cyclenote	precise information on this longer cycle is lacking..hw 	t»e-c|ï-t»e-jøpos 	Elabqgl  	for a couple of week-cycles; for a while (approx. 2-3 seven-day weeks)..hw 	t»e jø p‰pos 	NPqgl  	one full cycle of days..hw 	jø p‰ vepos 	SVgl  	for a week to run its courseex  	y»a pø ve qh\a-n»î jø p‰ \o le exgl	How many weeks have passed since the child was born?..hw 	y»a pø ve jøpos 	LRC + Nrhgl  	the week after a child is bornex  	y»a pø ve jø p‰ \o l»a exgl	Has a week passed already since the child was born?.hw 	jøpos 	Pvgl  	[*experiential *particle]ngl 	1. have ever V'ed; have ever had the experience of V'ingnote	GL 4.311, 4.63(3)ex  	n\ø L»ah—u=—ø-ch»î c»a jø \o l»a exgl	Have you ever eaten Lahu curry?ex  	≥\a K»o-th»e÷ —ø m»a Ÿga qay jø «s—e exgl	I've never managed to go to Bangkok yet.ex  	m»a te jø, m»a m\ø jø, m»a k»a jø, m»a «s—î exgl	"I've never done it, never seen it, never heard of it, so I don't know!" [impatient sentence uttered by an informant]..hw 	m»a Ÿga yø j\ø jøpos 	Adv + vV + Vh + Vv + Pvgl  	not have had the opportunity of practi